{"reference":"Jude 1","book":"Jude","chapter":1,"status":"pending","sections":[{"kind":"chapter_overview","title":"Overview","blocks":[{"id":"block_1","text":"Jude begins with blessing, then explains that a planned letter about “our common salvation” became an urgent appeal to contend for the faith once delivered to the saints. The letter’s central concern is communal fidelity amid people Jude accuses of distorting grace and denying their master. Its forceful language belongs to a conflict within the community and should not be treated as a warrant for coercion or violence.","verse_refs":["Jude 1:2","Jude 1:3","Jude 1:4","Jude 1:5-25"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_jude_001_138f23e3f37e3986d2f66c27"],"confidence":"high","interpretation_level":"inference"}]},{"kind":"interpretive_questions","title":"Points of Interpretation","blocks":[{"id":"block_2","text":"Several details in the opening require care. Jude calls himself a servant of Jesus Christ and brother of James, but does not call himself an apostle; identifying him with Jesus’ brother is traditional and historically plausible, not established by the letter alone. Verse 1 also has textual and grammatical uncertainty in the description of those kept for or by Jesus. Likewise, “the faith once delivered” most naturally refers here to the received Christian message and allegiance, rather than automatically including every later doctrine or institutional policy.","verse_refs":["Jude 1:1","Jude 1:3"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_jude_001_5951080778d656b0be5019ba"],"confidence":"low","interpretation_level":"disputed"},{"id":"block_3","text":"Jude’s accusations against the intruders should be read as the letter’s polemic, not as a full historical profile of the opponents. Their exact teaching, conduct, number, and self-understanding cannot be reconstructed confidently from these lines. Verse 5 also has a significant textual variant: some witnesses read Jesus, others Lord or God as the one who saved a people from Egypt. The “Jesus” reading would make the exodus claim unusually explicit, but the exact wording needs textual caution.","verse_refs":["Jude 1:4","Jude 1:5"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_jude_001_761afbbf96739e10274956d7"],"confidence":"low","interpretation_level":"disputed"},{"id":"block_4","text":"The examples of angels, Sodom, dreamers, and Michael are compressed apocalyptic and judgment imagery. Jude does not provide a complete account of the angels’ rebellion or a complete map of final punishment. The referents of “dreamings,” “lordship,” and “glories” are debated. Michael’s dispute over Moses’ body draws on a Jewish Moses tradition not preserved in this form in extant Hebrew Scripture; the scene emphasizes that Michael invokes the Lord’s rebuke rather than issuing a slanderous judgment himself.","verse_refs":["Jude 1:6","Jude 1:7","Jude 1:8","Jude 1:9"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_jude_001_bbd94626adcd0b7ed5427c0f"],"confidence":"low","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]},{"kind":"language_literary","title":"Literary Features","blocks":[{"id":"block_5","text":"Jude intensifies his denunciation through a chain of scriptural analogies and vivid metaphors. Cain, Balaam, and Korah form a threefold judgment comparison, though the precise feature drawn from each story is debated. The images of self-serving shepherds, waterless clouds, fruitless trees, wild waves, and wandering stars are escalating metaphors for the people Jude condemns, not statements about literal cosmology. Jude 14–15 also closely cites wording known from 1 Enoch 1:9; this shows authoritative use of that passage without deciding every question about 1 Enoch as a whole.","verse_refs":["Jude 1:10","Jude 1:11","Jude 1:12","Jude 1:12-13","Jude 1:14-15"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_jude_001_95128e449aa46d95ae2fae5f"],"confidence":"low","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]},{"kind":"things_easy_to_miss","title":"Details Worth Noticing","blocks":[{"id":"block_6","text":"Verse 12 probably places the condemned people within communal “love feasts,” though the expression is textually and lexically debated; it does not by itself describe a complete meal practice or formal office. Near the end, the manuscript tradition of verses 22–23 varies substantially in wording, sequence, and whether two or three groups are addressed. One well-attested form directs mercy toward people who doubt or dispute. These variations explain why translations differ in this pastoral passage.","verse_refs":["Jude 1:12","Jude 1:22","Jude 1:22-23","Jude 1:24"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_jude_001_196833d2b189ac5db2c0a752"],"confidence":"low","interpretation_level":"disputed"},{"id":"block_7","text":"The closing instructions are plural and communal: readers are to build one another up, pray, keep themselves in God’s love, and wait for Christ’s mercy. This stands alongside both the opening description of those kept for or by Jesus and the final praise of God as able to keep them from stumbling. Traditions differ over how these statements relate to human agency, perseverance, assurance, and apostasy; the letter places both communal responsibility and divine keeping in view.","verse_refs":["Jude 1:1","Jude 1:20","Jude 1:21","Jude 1:24"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_jude_001_5951080778d656b0be5019ba"],"confidence":"low","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]},{"kind":"why_it_matters","title":"Why This Matters","blocks":[{"id":"block_8","text":"Jude’s instruction to show mercy, rescue some from danger, and act with fear is a call for discerning mercy, not coercion, surveillance, forced confession, or spiritual abuse. The image of a garment stained by flesh uses contamination language and should not be converted into shame or rejection of embodied people. The doxology’s praise of God’s ability to guard and present the community blameless is a ground for hope and praise, not a perfectionist claim that faithful people never sin, doubt, relapse, or need care.","verse_refs":["Jude 1:22-23","Jude 1:23","Jude 1:24","Jude 1:24-25"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_jude_001_e0332081ef9e22c6ef914c1c"],"confidence":"medium","interpretation_level":"disputed"},{"id":"block_9","text":"Jude 4–18 and 2 Peter 2:1–3:3 share an unusually close sequence of examples and wording. That relationship is strongly supported, but the direction of dependence, the possibility of common sources, redaction, and dating remain debated. The parallels therefore illuminate a literary relationship without making either letter merely a copy of the other.","verse_refs":["Jude 1:4-18"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_jude_001_dee10d4eee7001869face5dd"],"confidence":"high","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]}],"generated_metadata":null}